\begin{abstract}
We present observations of the dark cloud L1204 in the 6 cm ($1_{10}-1_{11}$) transition of formaldehyde (H$_2$CO) obtained with the Onsala 25 m radio telescope. The H$_2$CO line is detected in absorption against the Galactic radio continuum across the cloud, allowing the distribution of dense molecular gas to be compared with previously mapped CO emission. The strongest H$_2$CO absorption is not coincident with the CO intensity maximum, but is displaced by approximately $10^{\prime}$ on the sky. This offset is significant relative to the telescope beam and indicates that the tracers are sampling different physical components of L1204. While CO emission outlines the more extended molecular material, the 6 cm H$_2$CO absorption preferentially traces denser, colder, or more effectively continuum-illuminated gas. The observed displacement therefore suggests chemical, excitation, or structural differentiation within the cloud rather than a single centrally condensed molecular core. We discuss the implications of the CO--H$_2$CO mismatch for interpreting molecular-line maps of dark clouds and for identifying sites of enhanced density in L1204.
\end{abstract}
